#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:421
msgid "posting:roles:assertion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:427
msgid "The role a person plays in the unit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:432
#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:476
#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:510
msgid "Text string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:442
msgid "``:assertion/posting:roles``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:447
msgid "``Commander``, ``Chief of Staff``, ``Second in Command``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:452
msgid "The most common value we record in this attribute is ``Commander``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:454
msgid "There are a variety of other roles a person can have including ``Second in Command``, ``Chief of Staff`` along with other less common entries. They will vary between countries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:456
msgid "In nearly all cases, the value entered in this field is taken verbatim from the source. ``Commander`` is coded as the person who is ultimately in charge of the unit. If a person is referred to as “the head”, “chief” or some other variation indicating that they are in charge of a :ref:`unit`, they should be regarded as the ``Commander`` for the purposes of entering a value in this attribute. Similarly, the head of government or head of state of a country should have their role recorded as ``Commander`` for their :ref:`posting`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:460
msgid "The ``Président de la transition`` of Burkina Faso is the head of state, along with the commander-in-chief of the security forces. In our data any person who has been the ``Président de la transition`` would have a posting to that :ref:`unit` with a :ref:`posting:roles:assertion` of ``Commander`` and a :ref:`posting:titles:assertion` of ``Président de la transition``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:462
msgid "Similarly, heads of academic or other security force institutions will sometimes be referred to as the ``Commandant``. In these cases, ``Commandant`` should be recorded in the :ref:`posting:titles:assertion` field, and their :ref:`posting:roles:assertion` should be recorded here as ``Commander``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:465
msgid "posting:titles:assertion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:471
msgid "A title held by a person that is separate from their rank."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:486
msgid "``:assertion/posting:titles``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:491
msgid "``General Officer Commanding``, ``Jefe Del Estado Mayor``, ``Vice Chairman``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:496
msgid "The range of titles will vary from country to country. For example, commanders of army divisions in Nigeria, who usually hold the rank of ``Major General`` also hold the title of ``General Officer Commanding``. The value placed in this attribute is taken verbatim from the source and not edited or standardized."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:499
msgid "posting:ranks:assertion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:505
msgid "The official rank of a person."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:520
msgid "``:assertion/posting:ranks``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:525
msgid "``General de División``, ``Teniente Coronel``, ``Air Vice Marshal``, ``Lieutenant Colonel``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:530
msgid "Ranks for security forces are usually established in law or policy or listed on websites of security forces themselves. Best practice is to find this citation first and then use this contextual citation for any other citation referencing a rank with an abbreviation, such as \"Lt. Col.\" for Lieutenant Colonel. In some countries ranks are refenced with a dash \"-\" rather than a space between words. Generally, SFM aims to avoid entering dashes or other additional punctuation into data and thus would enter ``Brigadier General`` rather than ``Brigadier-General``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/persons-postings.rst:533
